@echo off
cd /d "C:\Users\SubratoBiswas\trinamix-conversion-workbench"
echo Cleaning any git locks...
del /f /q ".git\index.lock" 2>nul
del /f /q ".git\HEAD.lock" 2>nul
echo Dropping tracked bytecode...
git rm -r --cached --ignore-unmatch backend/app/__pycache__ backend/app/services/__pycache__ backend/app/parsers/__pycache__ 1>nul 2>nul
echo Removing scratch files (QA renders, Excel lock files)...
rmdir /s /q "_qa" 2>nul
del /f /q "~$*.xlsx" "~$*.pptx" 2>nul
git rm -r --cached --ignore-unmatch _qa 1>nul 2>nul
echo Staging...
git add -A
echo Committing (no-op if nothing changed)...
git commit -m "Oracle's own column rules, read out of the templates instead of guessed. Every FBDI generation template carries a comment on each header cell stating the database truth for that column: BATCH_ID, NOT NULL, NUMBER (18), Batch identifier. The parser never read them. Worse, on the tabular templates, which the real Oracle generation workbooks are, it set the data type from whether one sample row happened to hold a number and left max length, format mask and allowed values empty. So the validation engine, which already knew how to check length, numeric, date and value set, had almost nothing to check with, and a live Supplier conversion reported exactly one kind of validation error across 156 fields. template_comments.py now mines them straight from the OOXML comment parts, handling the three dialects Oracle actually ships across the workbooks on file: labelled, as in Supplier, Column Name then Data Type then Description; bare, as in Customer, where NOT NULL lives on its own line; and value-list, as in BOM, where a Possible Values block follows the type. Across the seven templates that is 1,910 commented columns, of which 1,909 resolve a column name, 1,844 a data type, 1,351 a length, 252 a date mask, 74 a value set, 49 a NOT NULL and 855 an explicit instruction not to populate the column at all. Nothing is inferred; every constraint is a transcription, which is why it is safe to enforce, and the comment text is kept verbatim so a reviewer can check it against the tooltip. NOT NULL now makes a field required, so the mandatory gate covers columns whose header carries no asterisk. Precision and scale are new checks: nineteen digits in a NUMBER(18) is rejected by Oracle on every row, so it counts as a hard error, while excess decimals are a rounding warning. The do-not-populate instruction is reported when the output has a value there, and is also the authority for shipping the column blank. New column-rules endpoint and a Column rules panel in Output Preview, Cleansing. It aggregates by COLUMN rather than by row: eight thousand rows of row N too long is a wall, whereas 412 values exceed 240 characters, longest 380, with examples and the template's own spec beside it, is something an analyst can act on. Mandatory-with-no-value stops generation; the rest report. The denominator is honest: a column the template says nothing about is counted as not checkable, not as checked and clean, so the bundled Item workbook, which has no comments at all, says so instead of reading as fully verified. Also fixed, because both blocked the request outright: Oracle's own SupplierSiteImportTemplate carries one out-of-spec font family value and plain openpyxl refuses the entire workbook over it, so uploading a template the analyst actually has failed with a misleading invalid XML message. There is now a tolerant loader that clamps only the offending attribute and leaves the template's real formatting intact, which matters because that formatting is what template fill hands back; the stored copy is repaired too, so every consumer works. And the Supplier Bank workbook ships three CamelCase shadow copies of its interface sheets, which the macro writes into when it builds the CSV; importing them created 59 phantom target fields duplicating real ones under names no source column can match. Duplicate sheet views are now collapsed to the interface-table spelling. Two long-standing mismatches surfaced while wiring this up. The engine emitted Max Length Exceeded while both HARD_ERROR_TYPES and the explanation table said Exceeds Max Length, so an over-length value was never counted as a hard error and never got its plain-English fix. And the generation call site never passed allowed_values, so the value-set check had never once run at generate time even for columns where Oracle publishes the codes. The uploaded Supplier zip and Customer workbook are now the bundled set. Five of the seven were already content-identical to what shipped; Supplier Site brings one real new column, Override B2B Communication for Special Handling Orders. Rule coverage now stands at 136 of 156 Supplier Import columns, 183 of 211 Supplier Site, 1,235 of 1,254 Customer and 242 of 307 BOM. Item is the gap: the bundled Item workbook contains no comments at all, so its 291 rules come only from its Data Type row. Send the Oracle Item template with comments and it will match the others. Employee is HDL rather than FBDI, so its rules come from the signed strategy document, not a workbook. 382 tests over 34 files, run on the pinned pandas 2.2.3 and on 3.0.2."
echo Pushing...
git push origin main
echo.
echo ====== DONE - review the messages above, then press any key to close ======
